# Cap the CPU at 80 MHz instead of the IDF default of 160 MHz, but let power
# management scale the clock down (to the 40 MHz XTAL) when the CPU is idle.
#
# The device is network-/UART-bound and the SML UART runs at only 9600 baud, so
# the top clock buys almost nothing while roughly doubling dynamic current. A
# *variable* clock keeps the 80 MHz ceiling for the ESP-NOW send burst and drops
# to 40 MHz between cycles; automatic light sleep (tickless idle) then parks the
# CPU while the tasks block.
#
# Trade-off: on the ESP32 the Wi-Fi driver holds an APB_MAX lock while the
# interfaces are up, and light sleep can make the receiver miss the sender's
# connectionless ESP-NOW broadcasts. The minute-history backfill exists exactly
# for that case: anything missed over the air is pulled from the sender's own
# SPIFFS history afterwards, so enabling sleep does not lose data. If ESP-NOW
# ever proves unreliable on the bench, disable CONFIG_PM_ENABLE (and with it DFS
# and tickless idle) to fall back to the fixed 80 MHz behaviour. Hardware
# verification of ESP-NOW under light sleep is still open (see TODO.md).
CONFIG_ESP_DEFAULT_CPU_FREQ_MHZ_80=y
CONFIG_PM_ENABLE=y
# DFS at startup: max = DEFAULT_CPU_FREQ_MHZ (80), min = XTAL (40).
CONFIG_PM_DFS_INIT_AUTO=y
# Keep the light-sleep and idle paths in IRAM / optimised so the wake overhead
# (and therefore the average current) is lower.
CONFIG_PM_SLP_IRAM_OPT=y
CONFIG_PM_RTOS_IDLE_OPT=y
# Automatic light sleep ("tickless idle") in FreeRTOS.
CONFIG_FREERTOS_USE_TICKLESS_IDLE=y
# Wi-Fi sleep-related code in IRAM (lower power-save wake latency).
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_SLP_IRAM_OPT=y
